>
  <p>
    Estimates put the number of emails sent each day at over 300 billion, and <strong>roughly half of them are spam</strong>. What used to be a nuisance is now a large, automated industry.
  </p>

  <p>
    If important messages keep getting buried under coupons, scams and newsletters you never asked for, you're not alone. You don't need a new email address to fix it. Knowing how spammers get your address, keeping separate addresses for different purposes, and using <a href="/">disposable temporary email</a> for one-off signups will cut most of it.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Why Do You Get So Much Spam? The Root Causes
  </h2>

  <p>
    First, it helps to know how your address ended up on spam lists. Spammers rarely guess addresses at random. They usually get them from:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Data Breaches and Leaks:</strong> When an online store, forum or app is breached, its list of email addresses (and often passwords) ends up on criminal forums and spam lists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Public Web Scraping:</strong> If your email address is visible on your personal portfolio, LinkedIn profile, GitHub commits, or public forums, bots collect it and add it to mailing lists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data Brokers and Lead Resellers:</strong> Some free services, apps and sweepstakes sites sell their contact lists to brokers, who resell them to advertisers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dictionary and Brute-Force Attacks:</strong> Spammers try common names and prefixes (e.g., <em>john.smith@</em>, <em>contact@</em>, <em>info@</em>) on popular domains to find addresses that work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accidental Opt-Ins:</strong> Pre-ticked boxes at checkout or signup can sign you up for partner marketing without you noticing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    Spam isn't just annoying. It's also how most phishing, malware and email tracking reach you.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>
    The Best Defense: Separate Addresses
  </h2>

  <p>
    The most common mistake is using one address for everything, from online banking and taxes to free PDFs and airport Wi-Fi. Once that address leaks or gets shared, spam reaches everything.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fix is <strong>compartmentalization</strong>: different addresses for different levels of trust.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Tier 1, your primary address:</strong> only for essential, trusted services: banking, government portals, primary medical records, and key contacts. This address is never posted online or entered into promotional forms.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tier 2, an everyday address:</strong> Used for trusted subscriptions, e-commerce stores where you frequently order goods, and reputable social networks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tier 3, temporary inboxes:</strong> For one-time signups, free trials, coupon codes, forum registrations, Wi-Fi logins, and downloading gated whitepapers. This is where <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> comes in.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>
    How Disposable Email Prevents Spam at the Source
  </h3>

  <p>
    When a website wants to email you a code or activation link before you can read an article or download a file:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Copy the temporary address that TempoEmails creates when you open it.</li>
    <li>The verification code or link shows up in the inbox, usually within a minute.</li>
    <li>You complete your task without ever revealing your personal or work address.</li>
    <li>When the marketing emails start days or weeks later, they go to an address you no longer use.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Your real inbox never sees any of it. To learn how this works, read <a href="/blog/how-temporary-email-works/">how temporary email works behind the scenes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Smart Unsubscribe Rules: When to Click and When to Avoid
  </h2>

  <p>
    When junk arrives, it's tempting to click "Unsubscribe" right away. Depending on the sender, that can make things worse.
  </p>

  <h3>
    When It Is Safe to Click Unsubscribe
  </h3>

  <p>
    Legitimate businesses are bound by laws such as CAN-SPAM and the GDPR, and generally honor unsubscribe requests within a few days. Use the native <strong>"Unsubscribe" button</strong> provided at the top of modern email clients (like Gmail or Apple Mail), which uses the standardized <code>List-Unsubscribe</code> header rather than an external web form.
  </p>

  <h3>
    When You Must NEVER Click Unsubscribe
  </h3>

  <p>
    If the email comes from an unknown sender offering miracle cures, crypto schemes or loans you didn't ask for, <strong>don't click any link in it</strong>, including "Unsubscribe" or "Remove me". A click can:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Confirm that your address is real and someone reads it.</li>
    <li>Tell the sender when and on what device you read it, making your address worth more to them.</li>
    <li>Take you to a malicious download or a <a href="/blog/understanding-phishing-emails/">phishing site designed to steal credentials</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For these senders, mark the message as spam and delete it without clicking links or loading images.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Make Your Spam Filter Work for You
  </h2>

  <p>
    Email providers use machine learning to sort incoming mail, and your feedback helps it:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Always "Report Spam" instead of deleting:</strong> Deleting a spam email just hides it. Reporting it tells your provider's filter to catch similar messages, for you and for other users.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rescue False Positives:</strong> Check your Spam folder now and then. If real mail ended up there, mark it as "Not Spam".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Create Keyword and Domain Rules:</strong> Set up filters for spam that keeps getting through. For example, you can create a rule that automatically archives and marks as read any incoming message containing specific promotional keywords or coming from certain top-level domains (.xyz, .top, .icu) that you never legitimately interact with.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>
    The Truth About "Plus Addressing" (+tag)
  </h2>

  <p>
    Some people use plus addressing, such as entering <code>yourname+store@gmail.com</code> when signing up for services. While plus-addressing helps identify which company leaked or sold your address, it provides very limited spam protection:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Spammers can easily remove the <code>+tag</code> and keep your real address.</li>
    <li>All the spam still lands in your main inbox unless you set up a filter for every tag.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A disposable address from <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> keeps things fully separate, because nothing in it points back to your real address.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Protecting Your Email from Surveillance and Trackers
  </h2>

  <p>
    Marketers and spammers also want to know whether you read their emails. Most marketing emails contain invisible 1x1 tracking pixels that tell the sender when you opened the email, on what device, and roughly where you are.
  </p>

  <p>
    To block them, turn off automatic image loading in your email app, and see our guide on <a href="/blog/what-is-email-tracking/">what email tracking is and how to stop it</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Checklist
  </h2>

  <p>
    These take about 15 minutes:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Audit your public footprint:</strong> Search your primary email on Google and remove it from public forums, social profiles, and resume sites.</li>
    <li><strong>Use disposable email by default:</strong> Whenever an untrusted website asks for an email address to unlock content, test an app, or claim a one-off discount, use <a href="/">TempoEmails</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Secure your accounts:</strong> read our <a href="/blog/online-account-security-guide/">online account security guide</a> for passwords and 2FA.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn off automatic image loading:</strong> Prevent tracking pixels from verifying your active status to spammers.</li>
    <li><strong>Never click links in suspicious messages:</strong> Report spam immediately to reinforce your provider's defensive filters.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    With these habits and a disposable address for one-off signups, most spam stops reaching your real inbox. To see how TempoEmails handles your data, read the <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>
